TITLE:: NdefRackLib
summary:: the modules that come with the rack, each one an Ndef source function in a file
categories:: Libraries>NdefRack
related:: Classes/NdefRack, Classes/NdefRackModule, Classes/Ndef

DESCRIPTION::
A library module is nothing but an Ndef source function. Each one is a plain
file in the repository's code::modules/:: folder, so it can be read, copied and
changed without recompiling anything.

code::
Ndef(\osc, NdefRackLib(\verbos));    // the function itself
NdefRack.make(\turing);              // the same, under the next free name

NdefRackLib.keys;
NdefRackLib.categories;
NdefRackLib.keysIn(\filters);
NdefRackLib.help(\turing);
::

The library is read the first time something asks for it. A module file holds
one expression, an Event, and its file name is its key:

code::
// modules/drone.scd
(
	category: \oscillators,
	help: "three detuned saws",
	func: { Saw.ar(\freq.kr(55, spec: \freq) * [1, 1.003, 0.997]).sum * \amp.kr(0.1, spec: \amp) }
)
::

Its controls are the module's inputs. Give every one a code::spec:: and the
bars in the rack will reach where they should - see
link::Classes/NdefRackSpec:: for what happens when you do not.

An entry can also say code::display: \scope:: or code::display: \spectrum::,
and the window then draws a live picture of the module's own output inside it -
see link::Classes/NdefRackDisplay::. The library's code::scope:: and code::spectrum::
are functions that pass their input straight through, so the picture is of what
is patched into them.

Two more keys are words for the window only. code::outs: ["value", "gate"]::
names the output's channels, channel 1 first: the window writes them by the
output when they fit (code::value · gate::) and lists them in its hint.
code::labels: (scale: ["chromatic", "major", "minor"])::, one Array per control,
names the whole-number stops of a selector, the first for its lowest value:
code::scale:: at 2 reads code::minor::. Nothing about the sound changes, and a
malformed key is ignored. See link::#*outsFor:: and link::#*labelsFor::.

A third groups the controls of a module too long for one column into pages:
code::pages: [tone: [\freq, \oct, \shape], rhythm: [\burst, \rest]]::, a
name and its controls per page, first page first. The window uses it only when
the module has more rows than a page holds; a control on no page goes on a last
page, code::more::. Without it the window keeps the bands whole and fills its
pages evenly. See link::#*pagesFor::.

subsection:: Modules with voices

A module that can play several voices gives code::poly:: instead of
code::func:: - a function of the number of voices that answers the source
function. Every control written code::! n:: has a value per voice, and the UGens
make a copy of themselves per voice:

code::
(
	category: \filters,
	help: "a resonant lowpass; with voices, one per voice",
	poly: { |n| { RLPF.ar(\in.ar(0 ! n), \freq.kr(1200 ! n, spec: \freq), 0.3) } }
);
::

At one voice it is the plain module - code::0 ! 1:: is a single control. The
maker is asked once per number of voices and what it answers is kept, so
link::#*voicesFor:: can read the number back from an Ndef's source.
code::sliders: [\v]:: shows those controls as a slider per voice;
code::follow: \in:: makes the module take as many voices as the cable into
code::in:: brings. Voices are mono, a channel each; stereo is code::splay::'s job.

code::
NdefRackLib(\wave, 4);                     // the function for four voices
NdefRackLib.voicesFor(NdefRackLib(\wave, 4));   // -> 4
NdefRackLib.voiceKeys(\wave);              // the controls with a value per voice
::

subsection:: Modules on a bus

A module on a bus - one of BusManager's by name, or an index - gives
code::bus:: instead of code::func:: - a function of the bus's index, channels and rate answering the
source; for no bus it is asked with code::nil, 1, \audio::. The library's
code::bus:: module reads its bus:

code::
(
	category: \buses,
	help: "a bus - one of BusManager's by name, or an index of your own",
	bus: { |index, channels, rate| {
		var sig = case
			{ index.isNil } { DC.ar(0) }
			{ rate == \control } { In.kr(index, channels) }
			{ InFeedback.ar(index, channels) };
		sig * \gain.kr(1, spec: [0, 4, \amp])
	} }
);
::

link::#*busReader:: asks it for a bus - by name, as BusManager has the bus now, or
by index, width and rate - and keeps the function, so link::#*busFor:: reads the
bus back from an Ndef's source.

A module that writes to its bus says code::writes: true:: beside the
code::bus:: - code::send::, code::throw::, code::sendpan::, code::replace::,
code::blend:: - and one that reads it as well, code::insert::, adds
code::reads: true::. code::writes: \audio:: writes to audio buses only:
code::insert:: is a plain reader on a control bus. The flag makes the window and
the warnings say "writes to" where they say "reads" (link::#*writesBus::,
link::#*busVerb::), and a writer whose BusManager bus is taken away goes to its
stand-in rather than staying where it was. What a writer's function has to get
right: a fade of its own on what it writes, and a stand-in that writes nothing.

code::
// feed - in onto a bus, times level, and passed on
(
	category: \buses,
	help: "in added to a bus, times level, and passed on",
	writes: true,
	bus: { |index, channels, rate| {
		var kr = rate == \control;
		var in = if (kr) { \in.kr(0 ! channels, spec: [-1, 1]) } { \in.ar(0 ! channels) };
		var level = \level.kr(1, spec: [0, 2, \amp]);
		if (index.isNil) { in } {
			var env = EnvGate.new(i_level: 0, doneAction: 2, curve: if (kr) { \lin } { \sin });
			if (kr) { Out.kr(index, in * level * env) } { Out.ar(index, in * level * env) };
			in * env
		}
	} }
);
::

subsection:: Which module an Ndef came from

link::#*keyFor:: answers it by the function's identity. The table is never
emptied, so an Ndef made before a link::#*reload:: still knows what it is; that
is how the code export writes code::Ndef(\osc, NdefRackLib(\verbos)):: rather
than nothing.

subsection:: Modules that need sc3-plugins

A file whose function uses UGens that are not core says so on a line of its own:

code::
// requires: DFM1
::

Where the class is missing, the file is not loaded - it would not compile - and
its key is in link::#*skipped:: with the reason. The library that comes with the
rack has three: code::dfm1::, code::moogladder::, code::svf::.

subsection:: Conventions of the library

table::
## strong::what:: || strong::how::
## audio || mono from oscillator to filter to amp; stereo at code::mixer::, code::pan::, code::reverb::
## pitch || octaves at code::oct::, added to code::freq::; 1 is an octave up
## cutoff || code::oct:: moves it in octaves, added to code::freq::
## gates || kr signals, on above 0.5; a clock input steps on its rising edge
## modulators || code::lo:: and code::hi:: are the range they swing over
## wide outputs || the main signal on channel 1, where a single input reads it; code::pick:: takes out the others
## voices || mono, a channel per voice, until code::splay:: or code::sum:: brings them together
## listening || audio at code::in::, control signals out; levels in dB, nothing counts below code::floor::
## drums || hit by a gate into code::trig::, or by code::rate:: on their own
::


CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
METHOD:: at
The function of a library module, ready for code::Ndef(\name, ...)::.
ARGUMENT:: key
the module's key - its file name.
ARGUMENT:: voices
for a module with voices, how many: the function for that many, the same one
every time. A module without voices answers its one function, with a warning.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/Function::, or nil with a warning.

METHOD:: entry
Everything about a module: code::(key, func, poly, category, help, path, display,
sliders, follow, bus, writes, reads, speakers, outs, labels, pages, about, params)::.

METHOD:: outsFor
The names of a module's output channels, channel 1 first, from its code::outs::.
code::
NdefRackLib.outsFor(\seq8);     // -> [ value, gate ]
::
ARGUMENT:: key
the module's key.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Array:: of link::Classes/String::s, or nil.

METHOD:: labelsFor
The names of a selector's whole-number stops, from the module's code::labels::.
code::
NdefRackLib.labelsFor(\quantize, \scale);   // -> [ chromatic, major, minor, ... ]
::
ARGUMENT:: key
the module's key.
ARGUMENT:: name
the control's name; nil for every control's, an link::Classes/IdentityDictionary:: name -> names.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Array:: of link::Classes/String::s, or nil.

METHOD:: pagesFor
How the window pages a module too long for one column, from its code::pages::.
code::
NdefRackLib.pagesFor(\svf);   // -> [ (name: filter, names: [ freq, oct, res, level ]), (name: mix, ...) ]
::
ARGUMENT:: key
the module's key.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Array:: of Events code::(name:, names:):: - a String and
link::Classes/Symbol::s - or nil when the module does not say.

METHOD:: canVoice
Whether a module can play several voices - whether its file gives a code::poly::.

METHOD:: voicesFor
How many voices a library function plays.
ARGUMENT:: func
a function, an Ndef's source.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Integer:: - 1 for a module with voices at one - or nil for
a function that did not come from a code::poly::.

METHOD:: voiceKeys
The controls of a module with voices that have a value per voice - those that are
two wide when it is built for two.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Array:: of Symbols; empty for a module without voices.

METHOD:: sliders
The controls the module's file asks to be shown as a slider per voice.

METHOD:: follow
The input whose cable sets the module's number of voices, or nil.

METHOD:: canBus
Whether a module is on a bus, reading it or writing to it - whether its file gives a code::bus::.

METHOD:: writesBus
Whether a bus module writes to its bus - whether its file says code::writes: true:: - or, given a rate, to a bus of that rate: a module saying code::writes: \audio:: writes to audio buses only.
code::
NdefRackLib.writesBus(\send);               // -> true
NdefRackLib.writesBus(\bus);                // -> false
NdefRackLib.writesBus(\insert, \control);   // -> false
::
ARGUMENT:: key
the module.
ARGUMENT:: rate
code::\audio:: or code::\control::, or nil: whether it writes to any bus.

METHOD:: readsBus
Whether a bus module reads its bus: one that does not write, and one that says code::reads: true:: as well.
code::
NdefRackLib.readsBus(\insert);    // -> true
NdefRackLib.readsBus(\send);      // -> false
::

METHOD:: busVerb
What a bus module does with its bus, for a sentence - on a bus of that rate, when one is given.
code::
NdefRackLib.busVerb(\insert);               // -> "reads and writes"
NdefRackLib.busVerb(\insert, \control);     // -> "reads"
::
ARGUMENT:: key
the module.
ARGUMENT:: rate
code::\audio::, code::\control:: or nil.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/String:: - code::"reads"::, code::"writes to":: or code::"reads and writes"::.

METHOD:: busReader
The source of a bus module on a bus as it is now - reading it, or for a writer writing to it: its index, channels and rate are in
the function, made the first time they are asked for and kept. By name, the bus
as BusManager has it; a name BusManager does not know - or BusManager not being
installed - gives the module's silent stand-in, still bound to the name. By
index, the width and rate given; nothing follows it.
code::
f = NdefRackLib.busReader(\bus, \drums);
Ndef(\drums, f);
NdefRackLib.busFor(f);     // -> (name: \drums, index: 16, channels: 2, rate: \audio)
NdefRackLib.busFor(NdefRackLib.busReader(\bus, 20, 2));   // -> (name: nil, index: 20, channels: 2, rate: \audio)
::
ARGUMENT:: key
the module - code::\bus::, code::\send::, any with a code::bus:: maker.
ARGUMENT:: bus
the bus's name in BusManager, a bus index, or a link::Classes/Bus::; nil for none.
ARGUMENT:: channels
for a bus index: how many channels; 1 if not given.
ARGUMENT:: rate
for a bus index: code::\audio:: (if not given) or code::\control::.
ARGUMENT:: voices
how many voices, for a module whose maker takes them (code::sendpan::); 1 otherwise.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/Function::, or nil with a warning for a module that is not a bus module, and for a negative index.

METHOD:: busFor
Which bus a library function reads or writes to.
ARGUMENT:: func
a function, an Ndef's source.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Event:: - code::(name:, index:, channels:, rate:)::, name
nil for a bus index of your own, name and index nil for none - or nil for a function that is not a bus module's.

METHOD:: canSpeakers
ARGUMENT:: key
a module's name.
RETURNS:: whether the module pans among the speakers of a setup - it has a code::speakers:::.

METHOD:: speakerFunc
The source that pans among a setup's speakers, made the first time it is asked
for and kept - one function per setup, so link::#*speakersFor:: reads the setup
back from an Ndef. A name link::Classes/NdefRackSpeakers:: does not have gives
code::\stereo::'s speakers in a function of its own, bound to the name.
ARGUMENT:: key
a module's name.
ARGUMENT:: setup
a setup's name, an link::Classes/NdefRackSpeakers::, or nil for the default.
ARGUMENT:: voices
how many voices - each its own sound and place, all on the same speakers.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/Function::, or nil with a warning for a module without code::speakers:::.
The maker is given the setup as numbers, link::Classes/NdefRackSpeakers#-data::, so the function needs no class.

METHOD:: speakersFor
Which speakers a library function pans among.
ARGUMENT:: func
a function, an Ndef's source.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Event:: - code::(name:, setup:):: - or nil for any other function.

METHOD:: shape
What a module is for a number of voices, worked out in the language, without a
server: code::(rate:, numChannels:, controls:)::.
ARGUMENT:: key
the module.
ARGUMENT:: voices
how many.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Event::, or nil when the function does not build.

METHOD:: withVoices
The same module for another number of voices: a library function's sibling -
on the same bus, on the same speakers - or the function itself for a module
without voices.
ARGUMENT:: func
a library function, an Ndef's source.
ARGUMENT:: n
how many voices.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/Function::.

METHOD:: shapeOf
What a function's module is, worked out in the language: code::(rate:, numChannels:, controls:)::.
ARGUMENT:: func
a function.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Event::, or nil when it does not build.

METHOD:: codeFor
A library function as code that makes it again without the rack: a code::func::'s
own text, or its maker's text applied to what made it -
code::{ |n| { ... } }.value(16)::, a speaker setup as its numbers. What
link::Classes/NdefRack#*patchCode:: writes.
ARGUMENT:: func
a library function.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/String::, or nil when the text is not there.

METHOD:: maxVoices
The most voices a module can play: 16.

METHOD:: includes
Whether the library has that key.

METHOD:: keys
Every key, sorted.

METHOD:: categories
Every category, sorted.

METHOD:: keysIn
The keys in one category.

METHOD:: help
One line on what a module does.

METHOD:: about
A few sentences on what a module does - its code::about:: key, or its one-line help when it has none. The window's hint bar says it over a module's title and empty face.

METHOD:: paramHelp
What a control does, in words - the entry in the module's code::params:: key for it - or nil. Without a name, every control's, as an IdentityDictionary. The hint bar says it over the control's row.

METHOD:: describe
Everything about a module in one String: its help, its about, the names of its output channels, and every control with its default, its range and what it does - controls there only with voices marked so. Built without a server.
code::
NdefRackLib.describe(\lfo);
::

METHOD:: postHelp
Posts link::#*describe::. The window's right click menu has it as "about".

METHOD:: keyFor
The library key a function came from, or nil.

METHOD:: openFile
Open a module's file - in the IDE when there is one.

METHOD:: dir
The repository's code::modules/:: folder.

METHOD:: load
METHOD:: reload
Read code::modules/:: and every link::#*addFolder:: folder again. An Ndef
already running keeps its old function until you set it again.

METHOD:: addFolder
Read modules from a folder of your own as well, now and at every reload.
ARGUMENT:: path
the folder, code::~:: allowed.

METHOD:: folders
The folders added.

METHOD:: add
Add one module from code.
code::
NdefRackLib.add(\blip, { Blip.ar(\freq.kr(110, spec: \freq), \harm.kr(8, spec: [1, 60])) * 0.1 }, \oscillators, "band-limited impulses");
NdefRack.make(\blip);
::
ARGUMENT:: key
its key.
ARGUMENT:: func
the Ndef source function.
ARGUMENT:: category
where the window's menu puts it.
ARGUMENT:: help
one line.
ARGUMENT:: path
the file it came from, if any.
ARGUMENT:: display
code::\scope:: or code::\spectrum:: for a picture in the window; nil for none.
ARGUMENT:: poly
in place of code::func::: a function of the number of voices answering the
source function, for a module with voices.
code::
NdefRackLib.add(\blips, nil, \oscillators, "impulses, a voice each",
	poly: { |n| { Blip.ar(\freq.kr(110 ! n, spec: \freq), 8) * 0.1 } });
::
ARGUMENT:: sliders
the controls with a value per voice to show as a slider each.
ARGUMENT:: follow
the input whose cable sets the number of voices.
ARGUMENT:: bus
in place of code::func::: a function of a bus's index, channels and rate
answering the source function, for a module reading a bus or writing to one - see
link::#*busReader::.
ARGUMENT:: writes
true for a bus module that writes to its bus.
ARGUMENT:: reads
true for a writer that reads its bus as well.
ARGUMENT:: speakers
in place of code::func::: a function of a speaker setup - an
link::Classes/NdefRackSpeakers:: - answering the source function, for a module
with a channel per speaker; see link::#*speakerFunc::.
ARGUMENT:: outs
names for the output's channels, Strings or Symbols - see link::#*outsFor::.
ARGUMENT:: labels
names for selectors' stops, an Event control name -> names - see link::#*labelsFor::.
ARGUMENT:: pages
the window's pages for a long module, code::[name: [controls], ...]:: - see link::#*pagesFor::.

METHOD:: remove
Take a module out of the library. Ndefs made from it are not touched.

METHOD:: entries
Every entry, key -> Event.

METHOD:: skipped
The files left out, key -> why.


EXAMPLES::

code::
s.boot;
NdefRack.gui;

// the four the rack was made for
NdefRack.make(\verbos, \osc);
NdefRack.make(\lfo8, \lfo);
NdefRack.make(\walk, \walk).set(\lo, 0, \hi, 1);   // the range scan takes
NdefRack.make(\turing, \tm);

NdefRack.connect(\lfo, \osc, \levels);   // eight phases into eight partials
NdefRack.connect(\tm, \osc, \oct);      // the machine's melody, in octaves
NdefRack.connect(\walk, \osc, \scan);    // the window wandering
Ndef(\osc).set(\width, 0.25);
NdefRack.play(\osc);

NdefRack.stop(\osc);
::
